# TypeScript-Specific Rules (sample)

> This file is a sample with the `.example` extension. Activate by copying without the extension:
> ```bash
> cp .claude/rules/language-typescript.md.example .claude/rules/language-typescript.md
> ```
>
> Edit to fit your project. Delete sections you don't need.

## Type strategy

- `any` is forbidden. Use `unknown` + type guards when unavoidable
- Minimize `as` casts; prefer `satisfies`
- Function args and return types **must** be annotated (no inference-only)
- Use discriminated unions (`kind: "a" | "b"`) for tagged variants

## null / undefined

- Assume `strictNullChecks: true`
- Optional values as `T | undefined` (avoid `null`, except from external APIs)
- Use nullish coalescing (`??`) and optional chaining (`?.`)

## Error handling

- Only throw Error instances (no `throw "string"`)
- Custom errors: `class FooError extends Error` with consistent naming
- Async code must handle errors via `try/catch` or `.catch()`
- No silent swallowing. Either log, rethrow, or explicitly recover

## Naming conventions

- Types / interfaces: `PascalCase`
- Functions / variables: `camelCase`
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` (only for true module-scope constants)
- Boolean prefix: `is / has / can / should`

## File organization

- 1 file, 1 responsibility. Consider splitting beyond 300 lines
- Type definitions go in the feature directory's `types.ts` or near usage
- `index.ts` is re-exports only (no logic)

## Forbidden patterns

- `any`, `Function`, `Object`, `{}` types
- `@ts-ignore` (use `@ts-expect-error` + reason comment instead)
- `eval`, `Function constructor`
- Leftover `console.log` in production code (detected by `console-warn.sh`)

## Tests

- Test files as `*.test.ts(x)` or under `__tests__/`
- `describe` / `it` may use the project's default language
- Minimize mocks; don't depend on implementation details

## Recommended tools

- Biome (lint + format) or ESLint + Prettier
- tsconfig: `strict: true`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true`

## Concept alignment

- Project-wide conventions live in `project-config.md` §2 / §6
- This file contains TypeScript-specific details only (no language mixing)
- Violation-detection commands go in `project-config.md` §3
